# salon — the mirror salon, a rococo Spiegelkabinett in the south-east corner of the main block
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD — do not "tidy" any of it away.
#
# 1. TWO MIRRORS FACING EACH OTHER ACROSS THE ROOM, AND A SECOND PAIR DOWN ITS LENGTH.
#
#    `mirror` is rough=6, metal=252, and nothing else in this building paints it. The two long
#    walls of this room carry pier glasses whose faces are at z = -6.51 and z = -3.36 — EXACTLY
#    3.15 m (7 M) apart, exactly parallel, and the pairs at x = 9.45 and x = 12.15 are the same
#    0.90 x 1.80 plate at the same height on both walls, so each one looks straight into its
#    opposite number. That is the oldest hard case in rendering: a path that never terminates, an
#    image of an image of an image, and a bounce budget that has to be SPENT rather than exhausted.
#
#    IT IS BROKEN INTO PANELS AND THAT IS THE POINT. A single unbroken mirror wall facing another
#    is a degenerate corridor and tells you nothing. Here the plates are separated by piers 0.45
#    wide carrying gilt frames that project 0.09 into the room, so every reflected image is
#    interrupted by real geometry at every bounce, and the recursion carries something to look at
#    rather than a flat colour. A rococo room would have done it this way anyway.
#
#    The short walls carry a second opposed pair, at x = 6.39 and x = 14.76 — 8.37 m apart on the
#    room's long axis, so there are two mirror corridors at right angles at two very different
#    scales, and the chandelier stands in both of them at once.
#
# 2. FOUR HUNDRED SMALL REFRACTING SOLIDS IN FRONT OF A LIGHT.
#
#    The chandelier carries exactly 400 separate `crystal` solids at ior 1.62 — 384 drops in four
#    hanging tiers of octagon beads and hexagonal pendeloques, and 16 upright spires — between
#    0.06 and 0.10 m across, and EIGHT WAX TAPERS BURNING INSIDE THE RING THEY HANG FROM. Every
#    drop is a refraction event in front of an emissive, the caustic they throw is on a parquet
#    floor at rough=140 with a clearcoat, and their reflections are in nine mirrors at once.
#    Do not reduce the count and do not make the drops bigger; both of those delete the test.
#
# 3. THIN MATTER AT ONE AND TWO VOXELS, CUT AND PAINTED, SIDE BY SIDE.
#    At the contract's metre 32 a voxel is 0.03125 m. In this room:
#      cut         the astragal bead round every mirror, 0.03 by 0.045, one voxel by one and a half
#      cut         the reeds of the dado cap, 0.045 across on a 0.045 pitch, 1.4 voxels
#      cut         the rocaille scroll tails, tube 0.027, 1.7 voxels
#      cut         the shell reeds, grooves 0.03 wide and 0.045 deep — one voxel each way
#      cut         the sunk panel fields, 0.03 deep, one voxel of relief — CUT, not coloured
#      painted     the parquet marquetry, ebony lines 0.03 wide with NO GEOMETRY UNDER THEM
#      painted     the ceiling's gilt quadratura ring, 0.03 wide, likewise
#    The painted lines are the harder half — a denoiser has no edge to hold on to — and having
#    both in one room, in the same light, is what makes them comparable.
#
# 4. GRAZING LIGHT ACROSS A CLEARCOAT.
#    `parquet` carries lacquer=10 and `boiserie` carries lacquer=6. Three south windows and one
#    east window put low direct sun across 26 m2 of polished floor and up the panelling, and this
#    is the only place in the building a clearcoat lobe can be judged against a diffuse one at the
#    same incidence in the same frame.
# ================================================================================================
#
# THE MODULE, and where every number came from.
#
# M = 0.45, D = 0.90. The zone given is x 6.00 .. 15.10, z -6.60 .. -3.00, y 1.80 .. 6.00, and two
# of those four numbers CANNOT be module multiples: 15.10 is walls.clip's inner face (16.00 less a
# D wall) and -6.60 is the same on the south. So the room is set out on the 1.35 (3 M) grid the
# contract calls the spine of the elevation, and the leftovers are buried in wall thickness where
# nobody can measure them.
#
#   1.35   the bay. Every panel centre in this room is a multiple of it: 6.75, 8.10, 9.45, 10.80,
#          12.15, 13.50, 14.85 — and 8.10, 10.80 and 13.50 are where windows.clip already put the
#          three south windows, so the panelling was laid on the openings rather than beside them.
#
#   Horizontal
#     6.39    west wall face      the wall runs 5.95 .. 6.39; its east face is the room
#     14.76   east wall face      the wall runs 14.76 .. 15.15, lapping 0.05 into walls.clip
#     -6.51   south wall face     walls.clip's -6.60 plus 0.09 (M/5) of boiserie lining
#     -3.36   north wall face     my own wall, -3.36 .. -2.95, lapping 0.05 past the zone into the
#                                 east hall's block so the two are certainly one solid
#     3.15    mirror to mirror, which is 7 M exactly and the reason the north wall is where it is
#
#   Vertical
#     1.80    floor               podium_top, the contract's
#     2.70    dado cap            1.80 + 2 M, and the same line as windows.clip's sills
#     4.365   mirror head         the plate is 0.90 x 1.575 (2 M by 3.5 M)
#     4.455   frame head          and the rocaille cartouche stands astride it, to 4.635
#     4.68    cornice foot        4.95 - 0.27
#     4.95    cornice top / cove springing   1.80 + 3.15 (7 M), and doors.clip's side-door head
#     5.85    ceiling soffit      4.95 + 2 M, the cove being a quarter round of 2 M
#     6.00    top of the slab     the zone's ceiling; the piano nobile floor is at 6.30
#
#   THE COVE WAS 0.45 DEEP AND THE SOFFIT WAS AT 5.40, AND BOTH MOVED FOR ONE REASON: the
#   chandelier. Its lowest crystal hung at 3.915 and the clear air under it MEASURED 2.125 m — past
#   the brief's 2.10 for standing in and nowhere near the 2.40 a room wants along the line people
#   walk, which is the line this thing hangs on. It is 1.485 m tall from its bottom finial to its
#   canopy, so in a room 3.15 m in the clear it CANNOT be raised: there is nowhere for it to go. So
#   the room got taller instead. The cornice did not move — 4.95 is doors.clip's side-door head and
#   the mirrors' 7 M is measured off it — and the cove was deepened from M to 2 M, which lifts the
#   flat soffit to 5.85 and leaves 0.15 of slab under the ballroom's own 0.30. The chandelier went
#   up 0.45 with it and now measures 2.562 m of clear air under it.
#
#   Clear height under the cornice is 3.15, under the cove 4.05, and under the chandelier 2.562 —
#   and the doorway is 3.125 clear and 1.125 wide.
#
# WHERE THE DOOR IS. doors.clip cuts `doors_sd_void_e` at x 4.50 .. 6.30, y 1.80 .. 4.95,
# z -7.95 .. -5.70: the lesser south door, and it lands 0.30 inside my west wall. So the room is
# entered at its south-west corner, through an opening in the west wall, and this file cuts that
# opening itself as well — `salon_door_air` — so that the room reads correctly when it is measured
# on its own, and so that nothing is left standing between 6.30 and 6.39 where the door's own void
# stops.
#
# IT IS 1.125 (2.5 M) WIDE AND IT WAS 0.90 (2 M). The south jamb is not mine and cannot move: it is
# walls.clip's own south wall, whose inner face is z = -6.60, and the doorway is in the corner
# against it. So the opening measured 0.969 m in the assembled building — under the metre a person
# needs to walk through carrying anything — and the only jamb that could move was the north one,
# which went from -5.70 to -5.475. Measured after: 1.125 m. The west silk panel went 0.09 north with
# it so that there are still 0.065 of lining between the cut and the next thing, which is what every
# other cut end in this room stands clear by.
#
# WHERE THE WINDOWS ARE. Three in the south wall at x = 8.10 (round-headed), 10.80 and 13.50
# (square-headed), 1.35 wide, sill 2.70; one in the east wall at z = -5.40, the same square head.
# This file cuts its own reveals through the lining to exactly those openings and no further, so
# the panelling frames them instead of being cut in half by them.
#
# THE NUMBERS THAT ARE NOT DIMENSIONS. 1.60, 5.95, 15.15, -6.65, -2.95 are the 0.05 laps the brief
# demands, buried in floor and wall. 0.02 is every paint rule's `below=`, past the manifest's 12 mm
# of grain. 0.008 .. 0.015 are the arris rounds.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE DADO AND THE SUNK PANELS — one voxel of relief, all round the room
# ================================================================================================
#
# The dado is 0.90 (2 M) high and its cap lands on 2.70, which is windows.clip's sill line. The
# panel fields are sunk 0.03 into the lining — ONE VOXEL — and that is the shallowest relief in
# this room on purpose: halls.clip sinks its dado panels 0.0225 in a room lit by one sconce and
# reports that only a raking lamp finds them. Here there are three south windows and the sun rakes
# this wall for half the day, so a one-voxel step is a fair question rather than a lost one.
#
# `repeat` is placed by a translate so that the seed sits on 10.80 and the copies land on the 1.35
# grid — 6.75 through 14.85 — instead of on the world origin, which is in the rotunda.
let salon_dp_seed_s = box -0.54 1.98 -6.54   0.54 2.475 -6.45
let salon_dp_seed_n = box -0.54 1.98 -3.42   0.54 2.475 -3.33
let salon_dado_pans = union {
    intersection { translate { repeat { salon_dp_seed_s } x=1.35 nx=3 } 10.80 0 0
                   box 6.750 1.90 -6.60   14.625 2.60 -6.40 }
    intersection { translate { repeat { salon_dp_seed_n } x=1.35 nx=3 } 10.80 0 0
                   box 6.975 1.90 -3.50   14.175 2.60 -3.30 }
}

# THE TWO CORNER PIERS ON THE NORTH WALL. THEY WERE A PATCH AND THEY ARE NOT ONE ANY MORE.
#
# What this note used to say: windows.clip puts five ground-storey windows in the east front, at
# z = 0 and +-2.70 and +-5.40. The one at z = -5.40 is mine and this room is built round it. The one
# at z = -2.70 is not — its opening runs z -3.375 .. -2.025, which is 0.36 north of my room — but its
# REVEAL was cut 1.80 m into a wall 0.90 thick, from x 16.18 back to x 14.20, and that ran straight
# through the east end of my north wall. Measured then, at metre 8: the wall stood solid to x = 14.20
# and there was a hole 0.56 m wide and 1.80 m tall through it into the pocket behind. The two piers
# were how it was closed: 0.18 of pier standing IN FRONT of the cut, which stops at z = -3.375 and
# cannot reach past it.
#
# THE HOLE IS GONE. windows.clip cut its `wall_cut` from 1.80 to 1.00 on 2026-08-17 — the wall's own
# 0.90 and 0.10 of slack — so the east-front reveal now stops at x = 15.00 instead of 14.20, which is
# 0.10 past walls.clip's inner face and 0.15 into the lap at the very corner of my block. Nothing of
# this room is inside it.
#
# THE PIERS STAY, AND NOT OUT OF SENTIMENT. They cost the room 0.18 of depth at each end — 2.97 in
# the clear where the middle of the wall gives 3.15, and the widest thing a person is is 0.60 — so
# nothing about walking in here turns on them. What they buy is that the north wall reads as
# pier / five panels / pier, which is how a panelled wall of this date ends, instead of five panels
# running into a corner. They are now what they always looked like and were not.
#
# The pocket north of this wall is not mine and is still open: whoever ends up owning the fabric
# between this room and the east hall should look at the windows at z = 0 and z = -2.70, which at
# the time of writing open into nothing at all.
let salon_pier_ne = box 14.175 1.80 -3.54   14.76  4.95 -3.36  round=0.012
let salon_pier_nw = box  6.39  1.80 -3.54    6.975 4.95 -3.36  round=0.012
let salon_piers   = union { salon_pier_ne salon_pier_nw }

# The silk-hung fields, where the mirrors are not: the two corner piers and the two ends of the
# south wall, and a narrow one on each short wall beside its glass. Same 0.03 sinking.
let salon_sp_s_w = box  6.750 2.79 -6.54    7.29  4.365 -6.45
let salon_sp_s_e = box 14.310 2.79 -6.54   14.625 4.365 -6.45
let salon_sp_n_w = box  6.525 2.79 -3.60    6.840 4.365 -3.51
let salon_sp_n_e = box 14.310 2.79 -3.60   14.625 4.365 -3.51
let salon_sp_e_n = box 14.73  2.79 -6.42   14.82  4.365 -6.165
let salon_sp_w_n = box  6.33  2.79 -5.40    6.42  4.365 -4.68
let salon_silk_pans = union {
    salon_sp_s_w salon_sp_s_e salon_sp_n_w salon_sp_n_e salon_sp_e_n salon_sp_w_n
}

# THE REEDS OF THE DADO CAP. Three half-rounds 0.045 across on a 0.045 pitch — 1.4 voxels each,
# 1.4 voxels apart — running the whole 8.10 m of both long walls. Their centres sit ON the wall
# face so each stands 0.0225 proud and is half buried, which is what a reed is; a reed drawn whole
# in front of the face would be the 0.045 projection the cornice note refuses.
let salon_reed_s_one = capsule 6.525 2.61 -6.4875  14.625 2.61 -6.4875 r=0.0225
let salon_reed_n_one = capsule 6.975 2.61 -3.3825  14.175 2.61 -3.3825 r=0.0225
let salon_reed_s = translate { repeat { salon_reed_s_one } y=0.045 ny=1 } 0 0.045 0
let salon_reed_n = translate { repeat { salon_reed_n_one } y=0.045 ny=1 } 0 0.045 0
let salon_reeds  = union { salon_reed_s salon_reed_n }

# The dado cap itself, and the skirting, both run round all four walls as frames — the difference
# of two boxes — so every mitre is free.
let salon_dado_cap = difference {
    box 6.30 2.61 -6.60   14.85 2.70 -3.27
    box 6.48 2.55 -6.42   14.67 2.76 -3.45
}
let salon_skirt = difference {
    box 6.30 1.80 -6.60   14.85 1.98 -3.27
    box 6.48 1.75 -6.42   14.67 2.04 -3.45
}
let salon_dado = union { salon_dado_cap salon_skirt salon_reeds }

# What the panelling actually removes: every sunk field, cut 0.03 into the face it is sunk in. It
# goes into `salon_hollow` below and into the piers, so the relief is CUT and not merely coloured —
# which is the whole of load note 3's first half, and the thing that makes the painted marquetry on
# the floor a fair comparison rather than the only line in the room.
let salon_sunk = union { salon_dado_pans salon_silk_pans }

# ================================================================================================
# THE SHELL — one block, hollowed
# ================================================================================================
#
# Written as a single block rather than as five walls because walls.clip leaves the inside of the
# main block EMPTY (`walls_ring` is the difference of two boxes), so there is no interior structure
# here to hang a lining on and the room has to bring its own. One block also makes the part
# certainly one component before anything is added to it.
#
# It laps 0.05 into walls.clip's south wall at -6.65, into its east wall at 15.15, and 0.20 down
# into the podium at 1.60. Its north face at -2.95 is 0.05 past the zone's -3.00, into the east
# hall's own block, which stands at -2.925.
let salon_block = box 5.95 1.60 -6.65   15.15 6.00 -2.95  round=0.015

# The air of the room. 8.37 x 3.15 x 3.15 in the clear.
let salon_room_air = box 6.39 1.80 -6.51   14.76 4.95 -3.36

# THE COVE, and why it is a rounded box and not four mouldings.
#
# The ceiling opening at the springing is exactly the face of the cornice corona — x 6.615 ..
# 14.535, z -6.285 .. -3.585 — and the flat soffit above it is x 7.065 .. 14.085, z -5.835 ..
# -4.035, inset one module all round. A box grown by 0.45 and rounded by the same 0.45 IS that
# solid of revolution: at the top it is the core box, at 0.45 below it the core grown by 0.45, and
# between the two a quarter circle. Four cavettos mitred at the corners would have left four seams
# in the one surface in this room that has no seam in it.
# THE RADIUS IS 0.90 (2 M) AND THE CORE BOX MOVED WITH IT, so that the springing does not: grown by
# 0.90 the box still reaches x 6.615 .. 14.535 and z -6.285 .. -3.585 at y 4.95, which is exactly the
# face of the cornice corona and exactly where the old one sprang from. Only the top moved, from
# 5.40 to 5.85. The flat soffit above it is 6.12 x 0.90 — inset two modules all round instead of
# one — and the slab over that is 0.15, with the ballroom's own 0.30 on top of it.
let salon_cove_air = round { box 7.515 4.20 -5.385   13.635 4.95 -4.485 } by=0.90

# The doorway, cut to doors.clip's own head at 4.95 and its own jamb at -5.70. It runs from 5.90 to
# 6.615 and back to -6.65 so that it is cut past both faces of my west wall AND past the 6.48 the
# dado's west leg reaches — see THE PART, below, for what it costs to stop at the wall face. 6.615
# and not 6.52: at 6.52 it left a sliver of lining 0.005 wide against the silk panel's edge, which
# the sampler filled conservatively and then reported as 67 floating voxels. Every cut end in this
# room now stands at least 0.065 clear of the next thing, which is two voxels at metre 32.
let salon_door_air = box 5.90 1.80 -6.65   6.615 4.95 -5.475

# The window reveals, cut to exactly windows.clip's openings and no further. Square heads at 4.50,
# the round head springing at 4.05 and crowning at 4.725. The z of the south three runs -6.70 ..
# -6.45, which is 0.10 into a wall the window already opens and 0.06 into air.
let salon_win_s_b = box 10.125 2.70 -6.70   11.475 4.50 -6.45
let salon_win_s_c = box 12.825 2.70 -6.70   14.175 4.50 -6.45
let salon_win_s_a = union {
    box 7.425 2.70 -6.70   8.775 4.05 -6.45
    cylinder 8.10 4.05 -6.575  r=0.675 h=0.30 axis=z
}
let salon_win_e   = box 14.71 2.70 -6.075   15.15 4.50 -4.725
let salon_windows = union { salon_win_s_a salon_win_s_b salon_win_s_c salon_win_e }

# The firebox, cut into the north wall behind the chimney breast. 0.63 x 0.72, going 0.12 into a
# wall that is 0.41 thick.
let salon_fire_air = box 10.485 1.80 -3.62   11.115 2.52 -3.24

let salon_hollow = union {
    salon_room_air salon_cove_air salon_door_air salon_windows salon_fire_air salon_sunk
}
# ================================================================================================
# THE FLOOR IS LAID AND NOT POURED
# ================================================================================================
#
# The marquetry in here is PAINTED and stays painted — that is load note 3's harder half and the
# reason this room exists as much as the mirrors are. But a parquet floor is two things and only one
# of them was here: the pattern, and the fact that it is made of separate pieces that do not quite
# line up. So the blocks are laid SQUARE on a 0.90 (2 M) grid with the joints CUT 0.030 wide and
# 0.06 deep, and the painted lattice runs DIAGONALLY at 0.675 over the top of them, so the two
# systems never once coincide and both can be read for what they are — a cut arris a sampler can
# find, and a colour change it cannot, on the same square metre of the same floor under the same
# raking sun.
#
# And about one block in six has LIFTED, which is what a floor of this age does: a scatter of them
# stand 0.0375 and 0.030 proud — 1.2 and 0.96 of a voxel at metre 32 — on two grids of 1.80 and 2.70
# that do not share a period. Each is bedded 0.04 INTO the slab and sits inside its own 0.90 square
# with 0.075 of clearance to the joints either side, so it is a bump on a solid and can no more come
# loose than the dado can. `displace` would have said the same thing more smoothly and it was tried
# first, upstairs: a displaced slab with joints cut in it came back with ten and eleven floating
# voxels at metre 32 where a flat one with the same joints had none.
let salon_pq_zone = box 6.66 1.74 -6.24   14.49 1.92 -3.63
let salon_pq_gx = box -0.015 1.74 -7.00   0.015 1.92 -3.00
let salon_pq_gz = box 6.50 1.74 -0.015   14.60 1.92 0.015
let salon_pq_joints = intersection {
    union {
        translate { repeat { salon_pq_gx } x=0.90 nx=5 } 10.80 0 0
        translate { repeat { salon_pq_gz } z=0.90 nz=2 } 0 0 -4.95
    }
    salon_pq_zone
}
let salon_pq_pad_a  = box -0.36 1.76 -0.36   0.36 1.8375 0.36 round=0.012
let salon_pq_pad_b  = box -0.36 1.76 -0.36   0.36 1.83   0.36 round=0.012
let salon_pq_proud = intersection {
    union {
        translate { repeat { repeat { salon_pq_pad_a } x=1.80 nx=3 } z=1.80 nz=2 } 11.25 0 -4.50
        translate { repeat { repeat { salon_pq_pad_b } x=2.70 nx=2 } z=2.70 nz=1 }  9.45 0 -5.40
    }
    box 6.75 1.70 -6.15   14.40 1.90 -3.72
}

# THE LIFTED BLOCKS ARE UNIONED ON AFTER THE HOLLOW AND NOT BEFORE IT. They stand 0.0375 above the
# floor plane, which is 0.0375 INSIDE `salon_room_air`; put them in the block and cut the room out
# of it and every one of them is deleted by the room it is laid in — which is the same mistake, at
# a smaller scale, that took every sconce, bench and statue out of this building in D608.
let salon_shell  = union {
    difference { salon_block  salon_hollow  salon_pq_joints }
    salon_pq_proud
}
let salon_piered = difference { salon_piers salon_sunk }

# ================================================================================================
# THE CORNICE — 4.68 to 4.95, projecting 0.225 (M/2), and the cove springs off its face
# ================================================================================================
#
# Three members: a fillet 0.09 proud, a cyma reversa swelling from that to 0.225, and a corona
# fillet at 0.225. NO MEMBER PROJECTS LESS THAN 0.09. vestibule.clip measured a 0.045 projection at
# metre 32 and got back nought voxels — a moulding is only the swell in FRONT of its core, so a
# member that stands one voxel out is half a voxel of solid and misses every voxel centre it
# passes. 0.09 is 2.9 voxels and it is the floor for a projection in this room.
#
# Each cyma is given a core box behind it for the same reason: drawn bare it is a ribbon floating
# in the air 0.09 off the wall.

let salon_cor_s_fil  = box 6.39 4.68 -6.60   14.76 4.77 -6.42  round=0.008
let salon_cor_s_core = box 6.39 4.77 -6.60   14.76 4.905 -6.42
let salon_cor_s_cyma = cyma_reversa 6.39 4.77 -6.42   14.76 4.905 -6.285 run=x
let salon_cor_s_cor  = box 6.39 4.905 -6.60  14.76 4.95 -6.285 round=0.008

let salon_cor_n_fil  = box 6.39 4.68 -3.45   14.76 4.77 -3.27   round=0.008
let salon_cor_n_core = box 6.39 4.77 -3.45   14.76 4.905 -3.27
let salon_cor_n_cyma = cyma_reversa 6.39 4.77 -3.45   14.76 4.905 -3.585 run=x
let salon_cor_n_cor  = box 6.39 4.905 -3.585 14.76 4.95 -3.27   round=0.008

let salon_cor_e_fil  = box 14.67 4.68 -6.51   14.85 4.77 -3.36  round=0.008
let salon_cor_e_core = box 14.67 4.77 -6.51   14.85 4.905 -3.36
let salon_cor_e_cyma = cyma_reversa 14.67 4.77 -6.51   14.535 4.905 -3.36 run=z
let salon_cor_e_cor  = box 14.535 4.905 -6.51 14.85 4.95 -3.36  round=0.008

let salon_cor_w_fil  = box 6.30 4.68 -6.51    6.48 4.77 -3.36   round=0.008
let salon_cor_w_core = box 6.30 4.77 -6.51    6.48 4.905 -3.36
let salon_cor_w_cyma = cyma_reversa 6.48 4.77 -6.51   6.615 4.905 -3.36 run=z
let salon_cor_w_cor  = box 6.30 4.905 -6.51   6.615 4.95 -3.36  round=0.008

let salon_cor_gilt = union {
    salon_cor_s_cyma salon_cor_s_cor  salon_cor_n_cyma salon_cor_n_cor
    salon_cor_e_cyma salon_cor_e_cor  salon_cor_w_cyma salon_cor_w_cor
}
# AND THE TWO THINGS THAT MAKE IT A SECTION RATHER THAN A STEP AND A CURVE.
#
# A BED MOULD under the fillet: an `ovolo`, 0.045 tall, from the wall face out to the fillet's own
# 0.09 — so the profile now reads quarter round, dentils, cyma, corona instead of starting flat.
# It stops at 4.635, which is exactly where the rocaille cartouches over the glasses stop, so it
# runs behind none of them and through none of them.
#
# DENTILS, cut out of the fillet that was already there rather than stood in front of it. 0.09 wide
# on a 0.18 (0.4 M) pitch, the slot 0.045 deep into a member that projects 0.09, so every dentil
# keeps 0.045 of continuous core and the course cannot come apart however the grid falls. It is a
# subtraction: no volume added, no component added, and the one dead band in the profile becomes
# the enrichment an order of this date always has.
let salon_cor_s_bed = ovolo 6.39 4.635 -6.51   14.76 4.68 -6.42 run=x
let salon_cor_n_bed = ovolo 6.39 4.635 -3.36   14.76 4.68 -3.45 run=x
let salon_cor_e_bed = ovolo 14.76 4.635 -6.51  14.67 4.68 -3.36 run=z
let salon_cor_w_bed = ovolo 6.39 4.635 -6.51   6.48 4.68 -3.36  run=z

let salon_dent_s = intersection {
    translate { repeat { box -0.045 4.683 -6.465   0.045 4.767 -6.41 } x=0.18 nx=24 } 10.80 0 0
    box 6.39 4.67 -6.47   14.76 4.78 -6.40
}
let salon_dent_n = intersection {
    translate { repeat { box -0.045 4.683 -3.405   0.045 4.767 -3.46 } x=0.18 nx=24 } 10.80 0 0
    box 6.39 4.67 -3.46   14.76 4.78 -3.40
}
let salon_dent_e = intersection {
    translate { repeat { box 14.715 4.683 -0.045   14.66 4.767 0.045 } z=0.18 nz=10 } 0 0 -4.95
    box 14.66 4.67 -6.51   14.72 4.78 -3.36
}
let salon_dent_w = intersection {
    translate { repeat { box 6.435 4.683 -0.045   6.49 4.767 0.045 } z=0.18 nz=10 } 0 0 -4.95
    box 6.43 4.67 -6.51   6.49 4.78 -3.36
}
let salon_dentils = union { salon_dent_s salon_dent_n salon_dent_e salon_dent_w }

let salon_cornice = difference {
    union {
        salon_cor_gilt
        salon_cor_s_fil salon_cor_s_core  salon_cor_n_fil salon_cor_n_core
        salon_cor_e_fil salon_cor_e_core  salon_cor_w_fil salon_cor_w_core
        salon_cor_s_bed salon_cor_n_bed   salon_cor_e_bed salon_cor_w_bed
    }
    salon_dentils
}

# ================================================================================================
# ONE MIRROR PANEL, DRAWN ONCE — 0.90 x 1.80 of plate in a frame 0.09 wide and 0.09 proud
# ================================================================================================
#
# Local frame: x across the panel, y from the foot of the plate, z OUT OF THE WALL. Every one of
# the eight glasses in this room is this unit placed by a translate, and on the three walls whose
# normal is not +z, by a rotate first — which is why each of those is caught in a box of its own
# further down. `rotate` is given no bounding box by the field on purpose, and a union cannot cull
# a child whose box is everywhere.
#
# The plate straddles the wall face: -0.03 to 0.03, so the paint rule that names it catches the
# voxels AT the face and the mirror surface is the wall plane itself. That is what makes two of
# them exactly 3.15 apart and exactly parallel; a plate set into a rebate would have been 3.15
# minus whatever the rebate happened to be.
let salon_pgl_plate = box -0.45 0 -0.03   0.45 1.575 0.03

# The frame. A rectangular ring 0.09 wide, from 0.09 inside the lining to 0.09 proud of it.
let salon_pgl_frame = difference {
    box -0.54 -0.09 -0.09   0.54 1.665 0.09  round=0.012
    box -0.45  0    -0.30   0.45 1.575 0.30
}

# The astragal on the inner arris — 0.03 across, ONE VOXEL at metre 32, and 7.2 m of it in this
# room once all eight are placed. Drawn as a zero-thickness ring and rounded into a bead, which is
# doors.clip's and vestibule.clip's trick: a bead written as a torus mitres badly at a corner and
# a bead written as four capsules has four seams.
let salon_pgl_bead = round {
    difference {
        box -0.45  0     0.06   0.45  1.575 0.06
        box -0.435 0.015 0.05   0.435 1.560 0.07
    }
} by=0.015

# THE RAISED OUTER MOULDING, and it is what turns a frame into a frame. What was here was a flat
# ring 0.09 wide standing 0.09 proud, with a bead on the inner arris: two surfaces, both parallel to
# the wall, and in a raking light nothing between them. The outer half of that ring now stands 0.05
# further out again with a 0.020 arris on it, so the section is wall, fillet, raised moulding, sunk
# ground, bead, glass — five steps in 0.09 — and every one of the nine glasses in this room casts a
# line down its own outside as well as round its plate. It is written as the difference of two boxes
# for the same reason the frame is: a ring's four mitres come free.
let salon_pgl_out = difference {
    box -0.54  -0.09  -0.09   0.54  1.665 0.14  round=0.020
    box -0.495 -0.045  0.0    0.495 1.62  0.30
}
let salon_pgl = union { salon_pgl_frame salon_pgl_out salon_pgl_bead }

# ================================================================================================
# THE ROCAILLE — one asymmetric cartouche, drawn once, over every glass
# ================================================================================================
#
# A shell with fourteen reeds cut in it and two C-scrolls of different sizes, because a rococo
# cartouche that is symmetric is a Louis XIV cartouche. Local frame again: y = 0 sits INSIDE the
# top rail of the mirror frame, which is what everything here is anchored to — the shell, both
# scrolls and the tie all reach down past y = 0 into the rail, so the whole cartouche is one solid
# with the frame and the frame is one solid with the wall.
let salon_roc_body = intersection {
    ellipsoid 0 0.045 0  rx=0.225 ry=0.18 rz=0.05
    box -0.24 0.020 -0.06   0.24 0.24 0.06
}

# The reeds. 0.03 wide — one voxel — and cut only 0.045 into a shell 0.10 thick, so the shell is
# never severed and what the grid can miss is a softer arris rather than fourteen loose lobes.
let salon_roc_reed  = box -0.015 0.06 0.005   0.015 0.32 0.10
let salon_roc_reeds = intersection {
    around { salon_roc_reed } count=14 axis=z
    box -0.26 0.05 -0.02   0.26 0.30 0.12
}
let salon_roc_shell = difference { salon_roc_body salon_roc_reeds }

# The two scrolls, tube 0.027 — 1.7 voxels — and deliberately unequal: r 0.135 with 1.75 turns on
# the left, r 0.18 with 2.0 on the right. A `spiral` starts at radius r along the first cross-axis
# and winds in, so each one's outer end is at its centre plus r in x, and both of those land inside
# the tie below.
let salon_roc_scr_a = spiral -0.285 0.075 0.03  r=0.135 tighten=0.55 tube=0.027 turns=1.75 axis=z
let salon_roc_scr_b = spiral  0.240 0.090 0.03  r=0.15  tighten=0.50 tube=0.027 turns=2.00 axis=z

# The base rail of the cartouche: it reads as the scrolled bar a rococo shell sits on, and it is
# also what ties both scrolls and the shell into the mirror frame's top rail.
let salon_roc_tie = capsule -0.42 0.015 0.02   0.435 0.045 0.02  r=0.027

let salon_roc = union { salon_roc_shell salon_roc_scr_a salon_roc_scr_b salon_roc_tie }

# ================================================================================================
# WHERE THE GLASSES GO
# ================================================================================================
#
# SOUTH WALL, face z = -6.51. The three windows take x 7.425 .. 8.775, 10.125 .. 11.475 and
# 12.825 .. 14.175, which leaves two piers of 1.35 between them, centred on 9.45 and 12.15. Those
# two carry the trumeaux — the pier glasses — 0.90 wide with 0.225 of boiserie stile each side.
#
# NORTH WALL, face z = -3.36, blank because the east hall is behind it. Five panels on the 1.35
# grid at 8.10, 9.45, 10.80, 12.15, 13.50, of which the middle one is taken by the chimney's
# overmantel and the other four are glass. TWO OF THEM — 9.45 and 12.15 — ARE THE SAME PLATE AT THE
# SAME HEIGHT AS THE SOUTH TRUMEAUX AND DIRECTLY OPPOSITE THEM. That pair is the load note's first
# item and the reason the room is 3.15 deep and not 3.42.
let salon_gl_s_a = translate { union { salon_pgl  translate { salon_roc } 0 1.620 0 } }  9.45 2.79 -6.51
let salon_gl_s_b = translate { union { salon_pgl  translate { salon_roc } 0 1.620 0 } } 12.15 2.79 -6.51

let salon_gl_north_seed = intersection {
    rotate { union { salon_pgl  translate { salon_roc } 0 1.620 0 } } y=0.5
    box -0.70 -0.15 -0.30   0.70 2.35 0.30
}
let salon_gl_n_a = translate { salon_gl_north_seed }  8.10 2.79 -3.36
let salon_gl_n_b = translate { salon_gl_north_seed }  9.45 2.79 -3.36
let salon_gl_n_c = translate { salon_gl_north_seed } 12.15 2.79 -3.36
let salon_gl_n_d = translate { salon_gl_north_seed } 13.50 2.79 -3.36

# THE SHORT WALLS, and the second corridor. `rotate y=-0.25` sends local +z to world -x, which is
# what windows.clip's east front already relies on; `y=0.25` sends it to +x. Both glasses are
# centred on z = -4.05, so they look at each other down 8.37 m of room with the chandelier in the
# middle of it.
let salon_gl_east_seed = intersection {
    rotate { union { salon_pgl  translate { salon_roc } 0 1.620 0 } } y=-0.25
    box -0.30 -0.15 -0.70   0.30 2.35 0.70
}
let salon_gl_west_seed = intersection {
    rotate { union { salon_pgl  translate { salon_roc } 0 1.620 0 } } y=0.25
    box -0.30 -0.15 -0.70   0.30 2.35 0.70
}
let salon_gl_e = translate { salon_gl_east_seed } 14.76 2.79 -4.05
let salon_gl_w = translate { salon_gl_west_seed }  6.39 2.79 -4.05

let salon_glasses = union {
    salon_gl_s_a salon_gl_s_b
    salon_gl_n_a salon_gl_n_b salon_gl_n_c salon_gl_n_d
    salon_gl_e salon_gl_w
}

# The plates on their own, for the paint rule. Same eight places, same box, no frame.
let salon_pl_s_a = translate { salon_pgl_plate }  9.45 2.79 -6.51
let salon_pl_s_b = translate { salon_pgl_plate } 12.15 2.79 -6.51
let salon_pl_n_a = translate { salon_pgl_plate }  8.10 2.79 -3.36
let salon_pl_n_b = translate { salon_pgl_plate }  9.45 2.79 -3.36
let salon_pl_n_c = translate { salon_pgl_plate } 12.15 2.79 -3.36
let salon_pl_n_d = translate { salon_pgl_plate } 13.50 2.79 -3.36
let salon_pl_e   = box 14.73 2.79 -4.50   14.79 4.365 -3.60
let salon_pl_w   = box  6.36 2.79 -4.50    6.42 4.365 -3.60
let salon_pl_om  = box 10.44 2.79 -3.615  11.16 4.365 -3.555
let salon_plates = union {
    salon_pl_s_a salon_pl_s_b
    salon_pl_n_a salon_pl_n_b salon_pl_n_c salon_pl_n_d
    salon_pl_e salon_pl_w salon_pl_om
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE CHIMNEYPIECE — marble, on the north wall, on the middle window's axis
# ================================================================================================
#
# x = 10.80 is the centre window's axis and the middle of the 1.35 grid, so the chimney faces the
# window square and its overmantel glass looks straight out of it. The breast is 0.90 wide — the
# same as a mirror panel — and stands 0.225 (M/2) proud of the wall, which leaves the two piers at
# 10.125 and 11.475 clear for their sconces.
let salon_chim_breast = box 10.35 1.80 -3.585   11.25 4.725 -3.36  round=0.015

# The surround: jambs and a lintel round an opening 0.63 x 0.72, rounded like every stone arris in
# this building. The firebox itself is cut in `salon_hollow`, above.
let salon_chim_sur = difference {
    box 10.395 1.80 -3.645   11.205 2.61 -3.36  round=0.012
    box 10.485 1.75 -3.70    11.115 2.52 -3.30
}
let salon_chim_shelf = box 10.26 2.61 -3.72   11.34 2.70 -3.36  round=0.012
let salon_chim_cyma  = cyma_reversa 10.26 2.52 -3.645   11.34 2.61 -3.72 run=x

# The overmantel, on the breast face at -3.585 rather than on the wall face at -3.36. It is the one
# glass in the room that is NOT coplanar with any other, which is what a chimney breast does to a
# panelled wall and is worth having: it puts one reflector at an angle to the seven that are square.
let salon_chim_gl = intersection {
    rotate { union { salon_pgl  translate { salon_roc } 0 1.620 0 } } y=0.5
    box -0.70 -0.15 -0.30   0.70 2.35 0.30
}
let salon_chim_glass = translate { salon_chim_gl } 10.80 2.79 -3.585
let salon_chimney = union {
    salon_chim_breast salon_chim_sur salon_chim_shelf salon_chim_cyma salon_chim_glass
}

# ================================================================================================
# ONE SCONCE, DRAWN ONCE — ormolu, two branches, two wax tapers, two flames
# ================================================================================================
#
# Local frame: x across, y from the foot of the backplate, z out of the wall. Six of these, twelve
# tapers. The arms are capsules at r 0.045 — 2.9 voxels — and a capsule is one continuous solid from
# end to end, so a branch cannot come apart the way a chain of small pieces can.
#
# EVERY BRANCH IS DRAWN ON +x AND NOWHERE ELSE. `mirror` FOLDS the coordinate — it asks the shape
# at |x| — so a shape drawn on the minus side is asked for at a coordinate it does not occupy and
# comes back EMPTY, with no error and no warning. Written first with the arm at x = -0.18, this
# file's six sconces measured 146 voxels: six backplates, no arms, no nozzles, no tapers and no
# flames. That is the same picture the manifest's own note reports from playing as "i cant tell
# what they are", arrived at by a completely different route. The console legs below had it too.
let salon_scn_plate = intersection {
    ellipsoid 0 0.18 0.02  rx=0.105 ry=0.20 rz=0.05
    box -0.12 0 -0.06   0.12 0.38 0.05
}
let salon_scn_arm  = capsule 0.02 0.09 0.03   0.18 0.21 0.16  r=0.045
let salon_scn_noz  = cylinder 0.18 0.255 0.16  r=0.048 h=0.105 axis=y
let salon_scn_bob  = cylinder 0.18 0.30 0.16   r=0.066 h=0.054 axis=y
let salon_scn_wax  = cylinder 0.18 0.40 0.16   r=0.036 h=0.20 axis=y
let salon_scn_fire = ellipsoid 0.18 0.545 0.16 rx=0.045 ry=0.09 rz=0.045

let salon_scn_metal = union { salon_scn_plate  mirror { union { salon_scn_arm salon_scn_noz salon_scn_bob } } axis=x }
let salon_scn_taper = mirror { salon_scn_wax } axis=x
let salon_scn_flame = mirror { salon_scn_fire } axis=x
let salon_scn = union { salon_scn_metal salon_scn_taper salon_scn_flame }

# FOUR ON THE NORTH PIERS, at 8.775, 10.125, 11.475 and 12.825 — the middles of the 0.45 piers
# between the five north panels, which is where the brief asks for them. Turned a half turn so the
# branches come out of the wall and not into it, and caught in a box because of it.
let salon_scn_n_seed = intersection {
    rotate { salon_scn } y=0.5
    box -0.34 -0.06 -0.28   0.34 0.72 0.12
}
let salon_scn_n_a = translate { salon_scn_n_seed }  8.775 2.925 -3.36
let salon_scn_n_b = translate { salon_scn_n_seed } 10.125 2.925 -3.36
let salon_scn_n_c = translate { salon_scn_n_seed } 11.475 2.925 -3.36
let salon_scn_n_d = translate { salon_scn_n_seed } 12.825 2.925 -3.36

# TWO GIRANDOLES ON THE SOUTH TRUMEAUX — a sconce standing in front of a pier glass, which is what
# a Spiegelkabinett is for and what doubles its light. It costs a little of the plate and buys a
# flame with its own reflection 3.15 m away in the opposite mirror, and then that reflection's
# reflection. Do not move these onto the stiles to keep the glass whole.
let salon_scn_s_a = translate { salon_scn }  9.45 2.925 -6.51
let salon_scn_s_b = translate { salon_scn } 12.15 2.925 -6.51

let salon_sconces = union {
    salon_scn_n_a salon_scn_n_b salon_scn_n_c salon_scn_n_d salon_scn_s_a salon_scn_s_b
}
# The two lists the paint rules need, in the same six places.
let salon_scn_tapers = union {
    translate { intersection { rotate { salon_scn_taper } y=0.5  box -0.34 -0.06 -0.28 0.34 0.72 0.12 } }  8.775 2.925 -3.36
    translate { intersection { rotate { salon_scn_taper } y=0.5  box -0.34 -0.06 -0.28 0.34 0.72 0.12 } } 10.125 2.925 -3.36
    translate { intersection { rotate { salon_scn_taper } y=0.5  box -0.34 -0.06 -0.28 0.34 0.72 0.12 } } 11.475 2.925 -3.36
    translate { intersection { rotate { salon_scn_taper } y=0.5  box -0.34 -0.06 -0.28 0.34 0.72 0.12 } } 12.825 2.925 -3.36
    translate { salon_scn_taper }  9.45 2.925 -6.51
    translate { salon_scn_taper } 12.15 2.925 -6.51
}
let salon_scn_flames = union {
    translate { intersection { rotate { salon_scn_flame } y=0.5  box -0.34 -0.06 -0.28 0.34 0.72 0.12 } }  8.775 2.925 -3.36
    translate { intersection { rotate { salon_scn_flame } y=0.5  box -0.34 -0.06 -0.28 0.34 0.72 0.12 } } 10.125 2.925 -3.36
    translate { intersection { rotate { salon_scn_flame } y=0.5  box -0.34 -0.06 -0.28 0.34 0.72 0.12 } } 11.475 2.925 -3.36
    translate { intersection { rotate { salon_scn_flame } y=0.5  box -0.34 -0.06 -0.28 0.34 0.72 0.12 } } 12.825 2.925 -3.36
    translate { salon_scn_flame }  9.45 2.925 -6.51
    translate { salon_scn_flame } 12.15 2.925 -6.51
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE CHANDELIER — 400 crystal solids and eight flames, on the centre line
# ================================================================================================
#
# Built about the origin because `around` turns about the y axis through it, and translated to
# (10.80, -4.95) — the middle window's axis and the room's own centre line to within 0.015 — with
# no dy, because every height in it is absolute. Every one of those heights went up 0.45 when the
# cove was deepened; see the section note at the top of this file for why. The whole thing is caught in a box before the
# translate: `around` is a rotation and a rotation reports infinite bounds, and windows.clip
# measured what that costs when it is left bare (705 million field evaluations for one part).
#
# WHY THE DROPS ARE PRISMS AND NOT BEADS ON WIRES. A chain of small solids threaded on a wire is
# how a chandelier is made and it is not how one can be VOXELISED: a wire thin enough to look like
# a wire is under a voxel, so at metre 32 the drops let go of it and the part reports four hundred
# floating components. Here each string is a stack of octagonal prisms whose intervals OVERLAP, so
# the string is one continuous solid from the ring it hangs on to its pendeloque — and the ring is
# one solid with its spoke, the spoke with the stem, and the stem with the ceiling. Four hundred
# separate solids in the source, one component on the grid. The facets are kept by turning every
# other prism by half a facet, which is what makes a cut drop twinkle rather than a turned one.
#
# Sizes: beads 0.045 circumradius (0.083 across the flats, 2.7 voxels), pendeloques 0.030 (0.052
# across, 1.7 voxels) and 0.10 long. Both inside the brief's 0.05 .. 0.09.

# EVERY HEIGHT IN IT WENT UP 0.45 WITH THE CEILING. See the section note at the top: the clear air
# under the lowest crystal measured 2.125 m and now measures 2.562 m.
#
# AND THE STEM IS A TURNED BALUSTER RATHER THAN A PIPE WITH A BALL ON IT. It was one cylinder and
# one sphere: a shape with two radii in it and no profile at all, on the axis of a room with nine
# mirrors in it, so it is reflected eight times over and every one of those is the same pipe. It is
# a `revolve` of a seven-member section now — a vase, two knops and a shaft — which is exact, and
# the section never goes under 0.12 of radius, so nothing about how it survives a coarse grid has
# changed. THE `union { }` INSIDE THE BRACES IS NOT OPTIONAL: `revolve` is a one-child operation
# and takes only the first shape in the block, silently. The ballroom's stove urn had been a plain
# disc for exactly that reason and nobody could see it.
let salon_ch_canopy = cylinder 0 5.76 0  r=0.135 h=0.18 axis=y
let salon_ch_stem   = revolve 0 4.59 0 { union {
    box 0 0.00 -1   0.200 0.10 1
    box 0 0.10 -1   0.150 0.22 1
    box 0 0.22 -1   0.120 0.42 1
    box 0 0.42 -1   0.185 0.52 1
    box 0 0.52 -1   0.120 0.78 1
    box 0 0.78 -1   0.165 0.87 1
    box 0 0.87 -1   0.120 1.14 1
} } axis=y
let salon_ch_ball   = sphere   0 4.59 0  r=0.15
let salon_ch_fin_a  = sphere   0 4.47 0  r=0.09
let salon_ch_fin_b  = sphere   0 4.41 0  r=0.045

let salon_ch_ring_a = torus 0 5.31 0  ring=0.45  tube=0.048 axis=y
let salon_ch_ring_b = torus 0 5.04 0  ring=0.36  tube=0.042 axis=y
let salon_ch_ring_c = torus 0 4.77 0  ring=0.225 tube=0.042 axis=y

let salon_ch_spoke_a = around { capsule 0 5.31 0   0.46 5.31 0  r=0.048 } count=6 axis=y
let salon_ch_spoke_b = around { capsule 0 5.04 0   0.37 5.04 0  r=0.045 } count=4 axis=y
let salon_ch_spoke_c = around { capsule 0 4.77 0   0.235 4.77 0 r=0.045 } count=4 axis=y

# The eight candles, on the corona itself. They are INSIDE the ring the drops hang from, so every
# tier below is lit from above and behind — which is where the caustic on the parquet comes from.
# THE BOBECHE IS TURNED. A socket, a swell and a flared lip, revolved about its own axis at radius
# 0.45 — where two stacked cylinders were a nozzle with a coin on top of it. Every member of the
# section is 0.040 tall, which is 1.28 voxels at metre 32: written 0.020 and 0.022 tall, the same
# three members in the ballroom shed their outer rim and came back as SEVENTY-TWO floating
# fragments of two and three voxels, one ring per candle.
let salon_ch_pan   = revolve 0.45 5.31 0 { union {
    box 0 0.000 -1   0.052 0.040 1
    box 0 0.040 -1   0.082 0.080 1
    box 0 0.080 -1   0.102 0.120 1
} } axis=y
let salon_ch_wax   = cylinder 0.45 5.495 0  r=0.045 h=0.23 axis=y
let salon_ch_fire  = ellipsoid 0.45 5.640 0 rx=0.048 ry=0.09 rz=0.048
let salon_ch_cands = around { salon_ch_pan } count=8 axis=y
let salon_ch_waxes = around { salon_ch_wax }  count=8 axis=y
let salon_ch_fires = around { salon_ch_fire } count=8 axis=y

# ONE STRING: four octagon beads and a pendeloque, drawn at a radius and a height. Written out four
# times rather than made a function because the language has none, and the four differ anyway.

# tier one — 32 strings of 5 off the corona at 5.31, radius 0.45.  160 solids
let salon_ch_t1 = around {
    union {
        prism 0.45 5.310 0 r=0.045 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0
        prism 0.45 5.260 0 r=0.045 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0.0625
        prism 0.45 5.210 0 r=0.042 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0
        prism 0.45 5.160 0 r=0.036 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0.0625
        prism 0.45 5.085 0 r=0.030 h=0.10 sides=6 axis=y turn=0
    }
} count=32 axis=y

# tier two — 24 strings of 4 off ring B at 5.04, radius 0.36.  96 solids
let salon_ch_t2 = around {
    union {
        prism 0.36 5.040 0 r=0.045 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0
        prism 0.36 4.990 0 r=0.042 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0.0625
        prism 0.36 4.940 0 r=0.036 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0
        prism 0.36 4.865 0 r=0.030 h=0.10 sides=6 axis=y turn=0
    }
} count=24 axis=y

# tier three — 20 strings of 4 off ring C at 4.77, radius 0.225.  80 solids
let salon_ch_t3 = around {
    union {
        prism 0.225 4.770 0 r=0.045 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0
        prism 0.225 4.720 0 r=0.042 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0.0625
        prism 0.225 4.670 0 r=0.036 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0
        prism 0.225 4.595 0 r=0.030 h=0.10 sides=6 axis=y turn=0
    }
} count=20 axis=y

# tier four — 12 strings of 4 off the bottom ball at 4.59.  48 solids
let salon_ch_t4 = around {
    union {
        prism 0.12 4.590 0 r=0.045 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0
        prism 0.12 4.540 0 r=0.042 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0.0625
        prism 0.12 4.490 0 r=0.036 h=0.06 sides=8 axis=y turn=0
        prism 0.12 4.415 0 r=0.030 h=0.10 sides=6 axis=y turn=0
    }
} count=12 axis=y

# and 16 spires standing UP off ring C, which is the other half of what a cut-glass chandelier does
# with light: 384 + 16 = 400.
let salon_ch_t5 = around {
    prism 0.225 4.845 0 r=0.036 h=0.18 sides=6 axis=y turn=0
} count=16 axis=y

let salon_ch_box   = box -0.62 4.33 -0.62   0.62 5.87 0.62
let salon_ch_glass = translate { intersection {
        union { salon_ch_t1 salon_ch_t2 salon_ch_t3 salon_ch_t4 salon_ch_t5 }
        salon_ch_box } } 10.80 0 -4.95
let salon_ch_gilt  = translate { intersection {
        union { salon_ch_canopy salon_ch_stem salon_ch_ball
                salon_ch_fin_a salon_ch_fin_b
                salon_ch_ring_a salon_ch_ring_b salon_ch_ring_c
                salon_ch_spoke_a salon_ch_spoke_b salon_ch_spoke_c salon_ch_cands }
        salon_ch_box } } 10.80 0 -4.95
let salon_ch_wicks = translate { intersection { salon_ch_waxes salon_ch_box } } 10.80 0 -4.95
let salon_ch_lamps = translate { intersection { salon_ch_fires salon_ch_box } } 10.80 0 -4.95

let salon_chandelier = union { salon_ch_gilt salon_ch_glass salon_ch_wicks salon_ch_lamps }

# ================================================================================================
# THE FURNITURE — two console tables under the trumeaux
# ================================================================================================
#
# It is in `part_salon` and therefore out of `void_salon`, which is the whole point of the note at
# the bottom of clips/facility.clip: a room's void is the air of the room LESS the room's own
# stone, and a fitting standing in that air goes with it unless the room owns it. Every sconce,
# bench and statue in this building was deleted once for exactly that reason.
let salon_con_top   = box -0.495 2.61 -6.53   0.495 2.70 -6.14  round=0.012
let salon_con_apron = box -0.405 2.43 -6.51   0.405 2.61 -6.20  round=0.020
let salon_con_leg_a = capsule 0.36 1.80 -6.24   0.315 2.49 -6.30  r=0.036
let salon_con_leg_b = capsule 0.36 1.80 -6.47   0.330 2.49 -6.47  r=0.033
let salon_con_rail  = box -0.405 2.43 -6.53   0.405 2.61 -6.44
let salon_console = union {
    salon_con_top salon_con_apron salon_con_rail
    mirror { union { salon_con_leg_a salon_con_leg_b } } axis=x
}
let salon_furniture = union {
    translate { salon_console }  9.45 0 0
    translate { salon_console } 12.15 0 0
}
let salon_con_tops = union {
    translate { salon_con_top }  9.45 0 0
    translate { salon_con_top } 12.15 0 0
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE WINDOW SEAT — under the middle south window, and the only soft thing in the room
# ================================================================================================
#
# Everything else in here is marble, glass, gilt wood and lacquered floor, and a room that answers
# a light with nothing but hard specular lobes is only half a test. `damask` is rough=246 with
# sheen=10 and `silk` is 200 with 15; both are on the wall already as sunk panels 0.03 deep, which
# is a colour on a plane. This is the same two materials wrapped round a solid, three metres from
# nine mirrors and directly under the one window whose sun crosses the floor.
#
# AND IT IS NOT A ROUNDED BOX, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT MAKES UPHOLSTERY READ AS UPHOLSTERY: the
# squab is BUTTONED — three spheres subtracted on a 0.35 pitch, each pulling a dimple 0.18 across
# and 0.045 deep, which is 1.4 voxels of relief at metre 32 — its seam is PIPED with a bead 0.044
# across drawn as a zero-thickness ring and rounded, and it stands on TURNED legs, each a `revolve`
# of a five-member section rather than a stick.
#
# It sits under windows.clip's opening and not in it: the sill is 2.70 and the cushion tops out at
# 2.385. Its back rail is buried 0.04 into the south lining, so the whole thing is knitted to the
# wall along 1.26 m whatever the legs do at a coarse metre, and the legs go down to 1.72 — under the
# floor and under the 1.74 the board joints are cut to.
let salon_ws_back  = box 10.17 1.80 -6.55   11.43 2.34 -6.44  round=0.010
let salon_ws_rail  = box 10.17 2.16 -6.51   11.43 2.25 -6.06  round=0.012
let salon_ws_apron = box 10.21 2.01 -6.51   11.39 2.16 -6.10  round=0.010
let salon_ws_leg = revolve 0 1.72 0 { union {
    box 0 0.00 -1   0.055 0.05 1
    box 0 0.05 -1   0.034 0.11 1
    box 0 0.11 -1   0.052 0.18 1
    box 0 0.18 -1   0.030 0.26 1
    box 0 0.26 -1   0.046 0.32 1
} } axis=y
let salon_ws_frame = union {
    salon_ws_back salon_ws_rail salon_ws_apron
    translate { salon_ws_leg } 10.30 0 -6.15
    translate { salon_ws_leg } 11.30 0 -6.15
}
let salon_ws_cush = box 10.19 2.22 -6.49   11.41 2.385 -6.08 round=0.045
let salon_ws_pipe = round {
    difference {
        box 10.19 2.34 -6.49   11.41 2.34 -6.08
        box 10.22 2.34 -6.46   11.38 2.34 -6.11
    }
} by=0.022
let salon_ws_buttons = intersection {
    translate { repeat { sphere 0 2.455 0 r=0.115 } x=0.35 nx=1 } 10.80 0 -6.285
    box 10.30 2.36 -6.44   11.30 2.60 -6.13
}
let salon_ws_cushion = difference { union { salon_ws_cush salon_ws_pipe } salon_ws_buttons }
let salon_window_seat = union { salon_ws_frame salon_ws_cushion }

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================

let salon_built = union {
    salon_shell
    salon_piered
    salon_cornice
    salon_glasses
    salon_dado
    salon_chimney
    salon_sconces
    salon_chandelier
    salon_furniture
    salon_window_seat
}

# AND THE OPENINGS TAKEN OUT OF THE WHOLE OF IT AGAIN, AFTER THE TRIM IS ON.
#
# The shell is cut by `salon_hollow` where it is built, and that is not enough, because the members
# that stand ON the shell are added afterwards and they do not stop at the wall face. halls.clip
# spent a whole paragraph on this and it happened here anyway, so it is worth writing down twice:
# measured as `intersection { part_salon <the doorway> }` at metre 32, this file put **822 voxels
# of skirting, dado cap and cornice straight across its own door** — three bars, at ankle, waist
# and head height, in a doorway 0.90 wide. Nobody sees that in a component count, in a volume, in a
# material list or in a plan slice; the only thing that finds it is asking what of yours is inside
# the hole.
#
# The door cutter reaches 0.135 past the door's own void because the deepest thing standing in the
# opening is the dado's west leg at x = 6.48 and a void that stops at the wall face cuts the wall
# and leaves the trim. Same rule for the windows: the cornice foot is at 4.68 and the round-headed
# window at x = 8.10 crowns at 4.725, so without this the cornice fillet took a lens 0.48 wide and
# 0.045 deep out of the top of that arch.
let part_salon = difference { salon_built  salon_door_air  salon_windows }

# WHAT THIS PART REMOVES FROM THE BUILDING: the air of the room, its cove, and the last 0.30 of the
# side door's passage, LESS everything this file put back in either. Written as the difference
# against my own mass, and not as a list, for the reason vestibule.clip gives and for the reason
# the manifest's own note gives at length: the chandelier, six sconces, twelve tapers, two console
# tables and a chimney breast all stand in this air, and a void written as a bare box would cut
# every one of them away and leave nothing behind but the 0.09 of backplate buried in the wall.
# The window reveals and the firebox are NOT in it — they are cut out of my own block, above, so
# the manifest never has to know they exist and cannot open somebody else's wall with them.
let salon_air  = union { salon_room_air salon_cove_air salon_door_air }
let void_salon = difference { salon_air part_salon }

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Order matters: a later coat covers an earlier one. Shell first, then floor, then the mirror
# plates, then the gilding over their edges, then the hangings, then the fittings. `below=0.02` on
# every rule and never 0 — the manifest displaces the whole building by 12 mm at the end, and a
# test at zero misses the voxels displacement made and leaves a pale rind of limestone over the
# part.

# --- the shell -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
paint boiserie where=salon_shell   below=0.02
paint boiserie where=salon_piered  below=0.02
paint boiserie where=salon_cornice below=0.02
paint boiserie where=salon_dado    below=0.02

# The ceiling. The cove is gesso, the flat field is a painted sky in `silk`, and the ring between
# them is a gilt quadratura line 0.03 wide with NO GEOMETRY UNDER IT — the painted half of load
# note 3, put on the one surface in the room a grazing light never reaches.
let salon_cove_face = round { salon_cove_air } by=0.05
paint gesso where=salon_cove_face below=0.02

let salon_ceil_field = box 7.56 5.75 -5.34   13.59 5.93 -4.53
paint silk where=salon_ceil_field below=0.02

let salon_ceil_ring = difference {
    box 7.515 5.75 -5.385   13.635 5.93 -4.485
    box 7.545 5.75 -5.355   13.605 5.93 -4.515
}
paint gold_leaf where=salon_ceil_ring below=0.02

# --- the floor -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A slab of thin zones from 1.70 to 1.86, straddling the floor plane so every rule catches the
# surface and nothing under it.
#
# THE MARQUETRY IS PAINTED AND NOT CUT. The field is a diagonal lattice of ebony lines 0.03 wide —
# M/15, one voxel at metre 32 — struck both ways at 0.675 (1.5 M), with a walnut border and an
# ebony fillet inside it, and a lozenge of walnut under the chandelier where the caustic lands.
# There is no geometry under any of it. That is the point: the parquet's own arrises are cut (the
# skirting, the reeds, the sunk panels) and its pattern is not, in the same square metre, under the
# same sun, so the two can be compared rather than argued about.
let salon_fl_zone = box 6.39 1.70 -6.51   14.76 1.86 -3.36
paint parquet where=salon_fl_zone below=0.02

# AND THE PATH THAT HAS BEEN WALKED IN IT, which is the one thing on this floor that is honestly
# PAINT. A worn track is not a shape: it is the lacquer gone and the wood under it left bare, paler
# and rougher, in the places feet go, and it has no arris anywhere. `parquet` carries lacquer=10 and
# `oak` carries none and is rougher, so the two beside each other under the same low sun are a
# clearcoat lobe and a diffuse one at the same incidence in the same frame — which is load note 4's
# whole question, asked twice on one floor instead of once.
#
# It runs from the door in the south-west corner down the length of the room, and it is painted HERE
# — after the field and BEFORE the marquetry — because that is the truth about a worn floor: the
# ebony and the walnut are harder than the ground they are set into and they still read after the
# polish has gone off around them.
let salon_wear = intersection {
    union {
        ellipsoid  7.30 1.80 -5.90 rx=1.10 ry=0.30 rz=0.55
        ellipsoid  9.40 1.80 -5.20 rx=1.30 ry=0.30 rz=0.50
        ellipsoid 11.60 1.80 -4.95 rx=1.30 ry=0.30 rz=0.48
        ellipsoid 13.60 1.80 -5.05 rx=1.10 ry=0.30 rz=0.52
    }
    salon_fl_zone
}
paint oak where=salon_wear below=0.02

let salon_fl_field  = box 6.615 1.60 -6.285   14.535 1.96 -3.585
let salon_fl_border = difference { salon_fl_zone salon_fl_field }
paint walnut where=salon_fl_border below=0.02

let salon_fl_fillet = difference {
    box 6.60 1.70 -6.30   14.55 1.86 -3.57
    box 6.63 1.70 -6.27   14.52 1.86 -3.60
}
paint ebony where=salon_fl_fillet below=0.02

let salon_fl_bar   = box -0.015 1.70 -18   0.015 1.86 18
let salon_fl_diag1 = intersection {
    rotate { repeat { salon_fl_bar } x=0.675 nx=28 } y=0.125
    salon_fl_field
}
let salon_fl_diag2 = intersection {
    rotate { repeat { salon_fl_bar } x=0.675 nx=28 } y=-0.125
    salon_fl_field
}
paint ebony where=salon_fl_diag1 below=0.02
paint ebony where=salon_fl_diag2 below=0.02

let salon_fl_loz = intersection {
    translate { rotate { box -0.90 1.70 -0.90   0.90 1.86 0.90 } y=0.125 } 10.80 0 -4.95
    box 9.40 1.70 -6.30   12.20 1.86 -3.60
}
paint walnut where=salon_fl_loz below=0.02

let salon_fl_lozline = difference {
    salon_fl_loz
    intersection {
        translate { rotate { box -0.87 1.60 -0.87   0.87 1.96 0.87 } y=0.125 } 10.80 0 -4.95
        box 9.40 1.60 -6.30   12.20 1.96 -3.60
    }
}
paint ebony where=salon_fl_lozline below=0.02

# --- the glass ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE ONLY `mirror` IN THE BUILDING. Nine plates: two south trumeaux, four north panels, one
# overmantel, and one at each end of the room. Painted before the frames, because a rule with
# below=0.02 reaches 0.02 past its own shape and would otherwise silver the inner arris of every
# frame it touches.
paint mirror where=salon_plates below=0.02

# --- the gilding -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
paint gold_leaf where=salon_glasses below=0.02
paint gold_leaf where=salon_chim_glass below=0.02
paint gold_leaf where=salon_cor_gilt below=0.02
paint ormolu where=salon_reeds below=0.02

# --- the hangings ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
let salon_silk_face  = round { salon_silk_pans } by=0.03
let salon_dado_face  = round { salon_dado_pans } by=0.03
paint silk   where=salon_silk_face below=0.02
paint damask where=salon_dado_face below=0.02

# --- the chimneypiece --------------------------------------------------------------------------
paint marble where=salon_chim_breast below=0.02
paint marble where=salon_chim_sur    below=0.02
paint marble where=salon_chim_shelf  below=0.02
paint marble where=salon_chim_cyma   below=0.02
let salon_fire_face = round { salon_fire_air } by=0.05
paint iron where=salon_fire_face below=0.02

# AND WHAT THE FIRE HAS DONE TO THE MARBLE ROUND IT, and it is paint because soot is. A stain has no
# arris, no thickness and nothing for a sampler to find, which is the whole case for painting it and
# the whole case against cutting it. `tuff` is 150,142,124 at rough=250 against `marble` at
# 228,225,217 with translucency — a dead grey over a stone you can see into, which is exactly what
# fifty winters of smoke leave on a chimneypiece. It is kept under 2.86 so it stays on the surround
# and the shelf and never reaches the overmantel glass.
let salon_soot = intersection {
    union {
        ellipsoid 10.80 2.52 -3.55 rx=0.40 ry=0.34 rz=0.26
        ellipsoid 10.80 2.66 -3.62 rx=0.30 ry=0.20 rz=0.20
    }
    union { salon_chim_sur salon_chim_shelf salon_chim_cyma salon_chim_breast }
}
paint tuff where=salon_soot below=0.02

# --- the fittings ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
paint ormolu  where=salon_sconces      below=0.02
# THE TARNISH, which is the other thing paint is for. Fire-gilt bronze goes dark where a flame has
# stood on it every night for fifty years: a nozzle and a drip pan are not `ormolu` at rough=48 any
# more, they are `bronze` at 110 with the gold burnt off. The band catches the nozzles and the bobs
# and nothing else — the tapers and the flames are repainted below, which is why this can be a plain
# box round the top of the fitting rather than a shape cut to it.
let salon_scn_burn = intersection { salon_sconces box 6.39 3.15 -6.60   14.76 3.28 -3.20 }
paint bronze where=salon_scn_burn below=0.02
paint gilt    where=salon_ch_gilt      below=0.02
paint crystal where=salon_ch_glass     below=0.02
paint wax     where=salon_ch_wicks     below=0.02
paint wax     where=salon_scn_tapers   below=0.02
paint taper   where=salon_ch_lamps     below=0.02
paint taper   where=salon_scn_flames   below=0.02

# --- the furniture -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
paint ormolu where=salon_furniture below=0.02
paint marble where=salon_con_tops  below=0.02

# The window seat: a gilt frame, a damask squab, and the piping in the silk that is on the walls.
# The piping goes on after the damask because a later coat covers an earlier one and the bead
# straddles the cushion's own surface.
paint gilt   where=salon_ws_frame below=0.035
paint damask where=salon_ws_cush  below=0.02
paint silk   where=salon_ws_pipe  below=0.02
